From 9bd00d77b578112318498596702fdc379f6a564d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jfacemyer <1776340+jfacemyer@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 11:05:29 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] d2d1: Find the segment pairs to test in Close() with a
 grid.

Close() compared every segment with every earlier one in intersect_self
and resolve_beziers, and triangulate scanned the whole edge list once per
figure and removed duplicate vertices with a memmove each. A path of
138,242 curves in 3,911 subpaths (an engraving) took 38 s to close.

- A uniform grid over the segment boxes gives each segment the earlier
  segments whose boxes are within the margin, in ascending order, so the
  pair tests run on exactly the pairs, and in the order, of the full walk.
- resolve_beziers keeps the grid across splits: each split inserts one
  bezier after the one it split, so old positions map to new ones by
  counting the insertions before them, and the inserted beziers are
  checked on every query. The bezier list is updated in place on a split
  instead of being rebuilt and searched.
- insert_segments finds the lowest numbered live edge at a vertex from a
  per-vertex list kept by the edge setters, instead of a scan of all
  edges.
- Duplicate vertices are removed in one pass.
- The probe edges in each y bucket are sorted rightmost first, so an
  inside test stops at the first edge wholly to the probe's left.

Below 256 segments, vertices or edges the original walks are used: the
grid costs more than it saves there, and small paths (glyphs, ruler
labels) are closed thousands of times a second.

The results are unchanged: the filled vertices, faces, bezier vertices
and figures hash the same for that path, subsets of it and 66 random
self-intersecting paths; the bezier list kept in place matches a rebuilt
one after every split; and the d2d1 tests give the same 17521 results.
The engraving now closes in 1.1 s; small paths take the same time.
